{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices, especially beyond ~day 7. Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Over-optimistic rebound/mean-reversion: after a decline in the history window, the model predicts a recovery to low/mid-70s, but realized prices keep drifting down or stay lower. Damp mean-reversion strength (reduce pull toward recent average).",
    "Overshoot after short-term spikes: when the last observed price jumps (e.g., into 71–73), the model extrapolates/holds elevated levels; realized prices revert lower. Add a spike-fade rule: partially fade recent upward moves over the next 3–10 days.",
    "Trend underestimation on sustained downtrends: realized path shows a more persistent downward drift than the model. Add a negative trend component when the last 2–4 weeks slope is negative (carry-through rather than reversal).",
    "Horizon-specific error shape: early horizon (days 1–5) errors are modest; mid/late horizon (days 10–30) errors widen materially. Use horizon-weighted calibration: progressively stronger downward adjustment and volatility damping with longer horizons.",
    "Volatility too high in forecasts: model produces higher peaks and wider swings than realized. Apply amplitude compression (shrink deviations from the forecast mean), especially after day ~7."
  ]
}
